  {
   "id": 109,
   "type": "text",
   "title": "Application RED Metrics — NOT INSTRUMENTED",
   "gridPos": {
    "h": 20,
    "w": 12,
    "x": 0,
    "y": 6
   },
   "options": {
    "mode": "markdown",
    "code": {
     "language": "plaintext",
     "showLineNumbers": false,
     "showMiniMap": false
    },
    "content": "# Status: **NOT INSTRUMENTED**\n\nRequest Rate, Error Rate and Duration are still **not available from any UTM application itself**. No UTM service exposes a Prometheus endpoint.\n\n### Verified evidence (re-measured 2026-09-24)\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Prometheus `http_*` / `grpc_*` series from utm-* | **0** |\n| ServiceMonitors targeting utm-* namespaces | **1** — `utm-core/keycloak`, added by this work |\n| Services in utm-* namespaces | **85** |\n| Deployments in `utm-services` alone | **38** |\n\nThe single utm-* ServiceMonitor scrapes **Keycloak's JVM and database pool**, not application request metrics. It does not close this gap; see *11 - Keycloak Identity*.\n\n### Two partial substitutes now exist\n\nNeither is application RED, and neither is presented as such:\n\n| Substitute | What it genuinely gives | What it cannot give |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ingress edge metrics** — `nginx_ingress_controller_*` (**52** request series, **624** duration-histogram buckets, **2** hosts) | Rate, status class and latency for traffic crossing the ingress, per host and path | Nothing about service-to-service calls, which never touch the ingress; no internal error detail |\n| **Keycloak Agroal pool** — `agroal_acquire_count_total` | A *floor* on Keycloak request throughput | Only Keycloak, only requests that reach the database |\n\n### What is still unanswerable\n\n- What is the request rate to `operation-plan-service`?\n- What percentage of its requests return 5xx?\n- What is p95 latency for `conflict-detection`?\n\nIngress metrics answer these only for calls that enter through the ingress, and only by host/path — not per service, and not for internal traffic.\n\n### To close this gap\n\n1. Expose a Prometheus `/metrics` endpoint on each UTM service (Spring Boot: Micrometer + actuator; these are Spring Boot applications, so this is largely configuration).\n2. Add a ServiceMonitor per service — or one with a shared label selector — carrying the label `release: kube-prometheus-stack`, which is what the Prometheus CR selects on. The pattern is already proven three times in this cluster: `rook-ceph-mgr`, `ingress-nginx-controller` and `keycloak`.\n\nAn empty RED chart is deliberately NOT shown here: it would imply zero traffic and zero errors, which is a materially different claim from *not measured*.\n"
   },
   "pluginVersion": "12.1.1",
   "transparent": false
  },
  {
   "id": 110,
   "type": "text",
   "title": "Application Logs — AVAILABLE (Loki)",
   "gridPos": {
    "h": 20,
    "w": 12,
    "x": 12,
    "y": 6
   },
   "options": {
    "mode": "markdown",
    "code": {
     "language": "plaintext",
     "showLineNumbers": false,
     "showMiniMap": false
    },
    "content": "# Status: **AVAILABLE**\n\n**Datasource:** `Loki` (uid `loki-uid`), `http://loki.utm-statistics.svc.cluster.local:3100`\n**Shipper:** `alloy-logs` DaemonSet (`grafana/alloy:v1.20.1`), tails `/var/log/pods` on every node, CRI-parsed, labeled by `namespace`/`pod`/`container`/`node`\n**Retention:** 14 days (`336h`), compactor-enforced, 30Gi bounded PVC — see `loki-config.yaml` header for why bounded retention matters on this cluster\n\n### Verified evidence\n\n- Grafana datasource health check: `{\"message\":\"Data source successfully connected.\",\"status\":\"OK\"}`\n- `alloy-logs` pods `1/1 Running` on all 4 nodes (`node-004` included — hostPath/CRI collection has no Ceph CSI dependency, unlike Loki's own PVC)\n- Real cluster log data confirmed flowing, not just a manual test push — a `{namespace=\"utm-statistics\"}` query returned live entries labeled with pods from `utm-services`, `monitoring`, and other namespaces across multiple nodes\n\n### What this fixes\n\n- Log search and log-based panels are now possible via the `Loki` datasource.\n- **Jaeger and Tempo trace-to-logs correlation**, previously broken (`tracesToLogsV2.datasourceUid` pointed at the empty `es-logs-uid` index below), now points at `loki-uid` and resolves to real log lines.\n\n### Still open, separately\n\n`utm-services-logs` (uid `es-logs-uid`, index `utm-services-logs*`) is a **different** gap: app-level structured logs shipped to Elasticsearch, not Loki's container-log stream. That index is still genuinely absent (`_shards.total: 0` on `_count`) and remains its own, separate, not-yet-proposed piece of work."
   },
   "pluginVersion": "12.1.1",
   "transparent": false
  },
